# 1. The “self-improving software foundry” thesis

This is the most valuable thesis.

The cross-repository split is sensible:

```text
FinalBuilds2
= decision / memory / provenance / policy

Builda
= execution

AgentSEOLab
= experimentation

Dell / Dell2
= external-world verification sensor

DomainNameChecker
= specialized research/sensor

Hydra
= learned world-model projection
```

Your Foundry document gets the key principles right:

* immutable observations;
* provenance everywhere;
* version strategies instead of overwriting them;
* raw artifacts separate from derived graph state;
* evidence beating agent prose;
* reversible learning.

That last principle is especially important.

A system that says:

```text
"React is better"
```

is bad.

A system that says:

```text
strategy_architecture_17
promoted because:

n = 128 builds
success +13.2%
median build cost -8.1%
95% CI ...
experiment exp_287
models tested [...]
artifact evidence [...]
```

is incredibly useful.

Eventually the system doesn't merely remember *facts*.

It remembers **how it learned facts**.

That is the right endgame.

### Score: **9.5/10 thesis**

