# 5. VerificationEnvelope thesis

This might actually be your **second strongest thesis after the Foundry itself**.

The fundamental rule:

> software shouldn't need to trust the claim that something has been verified; it should receive independently checkable evidence

is exactly right.

The stack is well chosen:

```text
atomic claim
+
exact evidence
+
content hash
+
source provenance
+
verifier provenance
+
signed result
+
transparency receipt
+
expiry
+
deterministic replay
```

The standards aren't made-up architecture fluff either.

W3C VC 2.0 became a W3C Recommendation in May 2025. ([W3C][5])

SCITT became RFC 9943 in June 2026 and explicitly provides signed-statement transparency and receipts. ([RFC Editor][6])

Sigstore/Rekor genuinely provides append-only, auditable transparency with inclusion proofs. ([Sigstore][7])

And the research direction around claim/source-level provenance is real: ProvenanceGuard specifically finds that factual support and **correct attribution to the underlying source** are separate problems. ([alphaXiv][8])

This thesis can extend way beyond Dell:

```text
DealVerificationEnvelope
BuildVerificationEnvelope
DomainAvailabilityEnvelope
BenchmarkVerificationEnvelope
DeploymentVerificationEnvelope
ToolHealthEnvelope
PricingVerificationEnvelope
AgentSEOExperimentEnvelope
```

Then FinalBuilds becomes a **machine-verifiable evidence factory**.

That is powerful.

### Score: **9.5/10**

