# There is also a real ID bug / misleading claim

Latest projector documentation says:

> “SHA-256 truncated to 52 bits”

but the code actually does:

```js
hash.readUIntBE(0, 6)
```

Six bytes = **48 bits**, not 52.

And `TEST_REPORT.md` then says:

> “SHA-256 IDs (52-bit) — No collision risk.”

Two issues:

```text
52-bit claim = wrong
"No collision risk" = wrong
```

At 10,000 objects, 48-bit collision probability is tiny, but non-zero.

At much larger scales it becomes worth caring about.

There's also no reason your canonical identity should be a truncated database integer in the first place.

Use:

```text
canonical ID = UUIDv7 / stable string ID
```

and treat any Hydra integer ID as a purely internal projection key.

That fits your Pāṭala architecture lessons much better too.

